    82	## Agent runtime
    83	
    84	*Minds, chat, and the run primitive are one subsystem.*
    85	
    86	- **DECIDED · One mind per ticket.** One durable Hermes session (`session_key`), resumed for
    87	  each step. The same session is who the human chats with — chat and worker are the same agent.
    88	- **DECIDED · Connection: Option 3.** Reach the mind via the Hermes gateway run as a **stdio
    89	  subprocess** (JSON-RPC: `session.create` / `resume` / `prompt.submit`, streamed replies).
    90	  Child runs on Hermes's own interpreter, so no venv coupling to our server; structured
    91	  start/finish/error events. Not in-process (the old planner's mistake — it re-execs itself into
    92	  Hermes's venv and mounts the gateway); not a network daemon (that's later scale-out, a pure
    93	  transport swap on the same protocol).
    94	  - **VALIDATED (spike 01):** proven end-to-end against the real gateway —
    95	    `venv/bin/python -m tui_gateway.entry` speaks newline JSON-RPC; create → prompt → stream →
    96	    close → **resume the same durable key in a fresh process** with history intact; ~0.1s cold
    97	    start, agent build ~3.3s fresh / ~0.9s resume; 85 RPC methods enumerated; provider
    98	    prompt-cache survives process restarts.
    99	- **DECIDED · Per-session serialized input queue.** One in-flight run per `session_key`. Chat
   100	  messages, next-step prompts, and wakes are all producers into it; the executor drains it by
   101	  resuming the session when no run is live. Solves chat-mid-run and approval-lands-mid-run races
   102	  for free.
   103	  - **VALIDATED (spike 01) — now correctness, not just ergonomics.** The gateway's busy-guard
   104	    (`4009 session busy`) is **per-process only**; with the child-per-run topology (below) two
   105	    children could resume the same `session_key` concurrently. Our per-session queue is the only
   106	    thing preventing that — it is load-bearing correctness.
   107	- **DECIDED · Agent-operation primitive + role skills.** One primitive for how a mind is run: it
   108	  loads a **role-scoped Hermes skill** at kickoff (+ system prompt — see the caveat in
   109	  *Principles* — + tool/CLI surface), parameterized by role; the intelligence lives in the skill,
   110	  not the server. Skills: a **worker skill** (ticket agents — work a ticket, propose the next
   111	  step, raise a hand), a **rollover skill** (drives the rollover flow → *Days & rollover*), a
   112	  **main-agent skill** (global chat / board orchestration). Set at kickoff the way the kanban
   113	  flips a worker into worker-mode. Scope as v2-namespaced skills while proving v2; then they
   114	  become the primary way the planner operates.
   115	  - **VALIDATED (spike 01) — the mechanics.** Role skills load via `HERMES_TUI_SKILLS=<skill>`
   116	    in the gateway child's env: the SKILL.md is force-injected into the ephemeral system prompt
   117	    at agent build (proven live — the model named the skill and quoted its content with tools
   118	    forbidden; a bad name fails loudly pre-model-call). Granularity is **per gateway process**,
   119	    not per session — so a role = **one gateway child per run** carrying role env, which is
   120	    exactly the kanban worker shape. The primitive is
   121	    `run_step(session_key | None, role, prompt) → RunResult`: spawn child with role env →
   122	    `session.resume` (or `create` for step 0) → `prompt.submit` → drain events →
   123	    `message.complete` → reap child. The **system prompt** is the planner home's config
   124	    (home-wide baseline), not a per-session param; per-ticket context rides the next-step prompt
   125	    + per-run env (kanban's `HERMES_KANBAN_*` pattern). Slash menu for the UI =
   126	    `commands.catalog` (135 commands + categories + alias map). Details + follow-up tickets in
   127	    `spikes/01-hermes-linkage.md`.
   128	- **DECIDED · Dedicated planner `HERMES_HOME`.** Planner minds run in a dedicated Hermes home,
   129	  isolated from the owner's real `~/.hermes` (own `state.db` / `skills/` / config). Keeps planner
   130	  sessions out of the personal session list and lets us pin model / role skills / system prompt
   131	  for planner agents without touching the personal config. Costs a one-time provisioning step
   132	  (model config + creds + the v2 role skills synced in) — a small follow-up (spike 01 open
   133	  sub-Q1). Proven in spike 01: a fresh home fully isolates and fails loudly until creds are
   134	  provisioned; the v2 role skills install here.
   135	- **DECIDED · Per-ticket chat + global chat.**
   136	  - Per-ticket chat is the same one-mind session (above).
   137	  - **Global chat** is a planner-wide surface where you talk to the **main agent** about the
   138	    whole system (reorganize a sprint, broad orchestration), NOT scoped to one ticket/day. Today
   139	    chat is per-entity only (tickets, days); there is no global chat. It's the natural home for
   140	    the **rollover mini-review** and the **next-day-direction** exchange (both planner-level →
   141	    *Days & rollover*). Mechanically the same one-mind/chat model as ticket chat (Option 3 stdio
   142	    gateway + per-session queue), just a **global session** (the main agent) instead of a
   143	    per-ticket one.
   144	- Every mind receives only next-step prompts + human chat; the approval machinery stays
   145	  **invisible** to it (→ *Principles*).
   146	
   147	---
   148	
   149	## Scheduling & runs
   150	
   151	- **DECIDED · Two code-side systems, designed separately.**
   152	  - **System A — Readiness / poll.** Decides what is *ready* to be worked (dependencies met,
   153	    not blocked, nothing already running). It does no work — it only says "this one's ready."
   154	    Polls state, never the model.
   155	  - **System B — Set-off / run primitive.** Actually starts an agent on a step. Shared by the
   156	    poll (A) and the post-approval flow. Owns the ticket's run-status.
   157	- **DECIDED · Kickoff = step 0 (approving).** Don't treat starting a ticket as special — it's just
   158	  "step 0 approved." Both kickoff and approval feed System B: "set off step N of ticket X"; System
   159	  B never special-cases fresh vs continuing. Whether a fresh ticket auto-runs its first step or
   160	  waits for the human is governed by the **grant** (step 0 is the first advance), same as any later
   161	  step — no separate kickoff concept.
   162	- **DECIDED · System B is the writer; structured events bound each run.** System B launches the
   163	  agent (→ `agent_working`) and observes it end (→ `awaiting_approval` / `errored`); it never
   164	  lets the agent stamp status (→ *Principles*, *Tickets*). Option 3's structured events guarantee
   165	  the code always knows start and end; if it ever doesn't, the transport is structured wrong.
   166	  - **VALIDATED (spike 01) — topology.** System B spawns a **fresh gateway child per run**
   167	    (kanban-shaped), carrying role env; it resumes (or creates step 0), `prompt.submit`s, drains
   168	    to the single `message.complete`, then reaps the child — process-exit is a second, OS-level
   169	    end-of-run signal. `run_step()` (→ *Agent runtime*) is this primitive; kickoff = step 0
   170	    through it. ~1s spawn/resume overhead per step is noise against multi-minute steps.
   171	- **DECIDED · Poll efficiency.** Query only candidate tickets (a WHERE on status / readiness),
   172	  not a full-table re-scan-and-unpack every tick. (Implementation detail for the System A pass.)
   173	- **DECIDED · Fast path.** Keep the timer as a backstop; add a fast path so an approval / unblock
   174	  pokes System B immediately rather than waiting up to a full tick. (Detail for the System A pass.)
   175	- **DECIDED · "Can't proceed yet."** It's a **readiness** call in code — the poll simply doesn't
   176	  start the agent; there is no stop-condition on the agent (an agent never reports "blocked"; see
   177	  the REMOVE below).
   178	- **REMOVE · Agent can close a run `blocked`.** An agent never reports "blocked"; it only finishes
   179	  a step by offering its result for approval. "Can't proceed yet" is a code decision to not start
   180	  the agent (OPEN, above). Removing this deletes the respawn-forever loop (audit P1).
   181	- **REMOVE · Budget / per-tick concurrency cap.** Never asked for; adds slop plus a starvation bug
   182	  (counted per claim-attempt). No cost cap wanted now. (Any concurrency cap later — currently no;
   183	  revisit only if one is later wanted.)
   184	- **REMOVE · Timeout-as-failure.** Don't kill-at-30-min-and-count-a-failure with no liveness
   185	  check; two long-but-healthy runs shouldn't trip a breaker.
   186	- **REMOVE · Circuit-breaker / "failure."** "Failing" now = a run ends `errored` (crashed, or ran
   187	  but left no proposal). Errored **stops and surfaces** to the human; it does **not** auto-retry
   188	  (for now) — so there's no retry loop to cut off, hence no breaker. The breaker's columns
   189	  `auto_blocked` / `consecutive_failures` (earlier marked "deferred to the tail pass; not
   190	  decided") go with it. FUTURE: as real errors occur, decide case-by-case where auto-retry is
   191	  worth it.
   192	- **REMOVE · Full config re-parse every tick.** Read the one on/off setting, or read at boot —
   193	  not the whole YAML file every tick. (The made-up config knobs themselves → *Data model*.)
   194	- **DECIDED · Dispatcher machine-lock — KEEP.** Well-scoped, harmless; leave it.
   195	- **PARKED · Reclaim (kill-less / liveness-blind).** The one real question left: what if the agent
   196	  process dies in a way the code never cleanly observes (a hard machine kill)? Everything else is
   197	  handled by the code writing `awaiting_approval` / `errored` itself. → unblocked by a dedicated
   198	  reclaim / liveness pass.
   199	
   200	---
   201	
   202	## Tickets
   203	
   204	- **DECIDED · Status belongs to the ticket (the old "lock", reframed).** Replace the claim-lock +
   205	  separate `runs`-row split with the ticket's own status field, plus a "who/what is working it"
   206	  field. **Ticket status**: `empty` (nothing started) / `agent_working` / `awaiting_approval` /
   207	  `errored`. It just reflects where the ticket is; the UI reads this one field.
   208	- **DECIDED · Every run ends on `awaiting_approval`, never "finished".** An agent always ends a
   209	  step by leaving something for approval — it never declares itself done. "Done" is a *ticket*
   210	  state reached when a human approves the final step, not an agent-run state.

