   155	
   156	## 2 · Findings per question
   157	
   158	### Q1 — Transport: YES, works exactly as hoped
   159	
   160	- `venv/bin/python -m tui_gateway.entry` is a stdio JSON-RPC server: one request or
   161	  response/event per line; first frame is the `gateway.ready` event. Parent needs no
   162	  Hermes imports, no venv coupling — proven from system python3.
   163	- Full round trip proven: create → prompt → streamed deltas → complete → close → **resume
   164	  the same durable key in a brand-new process** → continuity → delete.
   165	- Session identity: `session.create` returns a live per-process handle (`session_id`,
   166	  8-hex) plus the durable `stored_session_id` (`YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS_xxxxxx`), persisted in
   167	  the active home's `state.db`. All later RPCs take the live handle; `session.resume`
   168	  takes the durable key (or a title) and returns a new live handle + full history.
   169	- Method surface (all 85, enumerated from `@method("...")` in `tui_gateway/server.py` and
   170	  spot-called): `session.create/resume/list/close/delete/status/history/title/usage/
   171	  interrupt/steer/branch/compress/undo/save/activate/active_list/most_recent/cwd.set`,
   172	  `prompt.submit`, `prompt.background`, `approval.respond`, `clarify.respond`,
   173	  `sudo.respond`, `secret.respond`, `commands.catalog`, `command.resolve`,
   174	  `command.dispatch`, `complete.slash`, `complete.path`, `slash.exec`, `cli.exec`,
   175	  `shell.exec`, `skills.manage`, `skills.reload`, `tools.list/show/configure`,
   176	  `toolsets.list`, `config.get/set/show`, `model.options/save_key/disconnect`,
   177	  `session.info` (event), plus voice/browser/billing/rollback/cron/plugins groups we
   178	  don't need. Slow handlers (`session.resume`, `slash.exec`, `cli.exec`, `shell.exec`,
   179	  `session.compress/branch`, `skills.manage`, …) run on an internal 4-thread pool, so
   180	  `session.interrupt`/`approval.respond` stay responsive mid-run.
   181	- Events observed live: `gateway.ready`, `session.info`, `message.start`,
   182	  `message.delta`, `thinking.delta`, `reasoning.available`, `message.complete`
   183	  (status `complete` | `interrupted`; `error` also possible per source), `error`,
   184	  `status.update`. Source also defines `tool.start/complete/generating`,
   185	  `approval.request`, `background.complete`.
   186	- Timings: **ready 0.10–0.15s; agent build ~3.3s fresh / ~0.9s resume; tiny prompt round
   187	  trip 2.6–8.4s** (gpt-5.5, high reasoning). Costs rode the owner's subscription
   188	  (`cost_status: "included"`); ~32k input tokens per step (full toolset + skill), with
   189	  provider prompt-caching working across process restarts.
   190	- Errors are clean JSON-RPC: `-32601` unknown method, `4007` not found, `4009` busy,
   191	  `4023` delete-while-active, `4090` session-slot limit (only if
   192	  `max_concurrent_sessions` is configured; it's `null` here).
   193	
   194	### Q2 — Skills at kickoff: YES — feasible today, per gateway *process*
   195	
   196	- Mechanism: set `HERMES_TUI_SKILLS=<name>[,<name>…]` in the child's env. Every agent
   197	  build in that process (create *and* resume) calls
   198	  `build_preloaded_skills_prompt(...)` (agent/skill_commands.py:564), which loads each
   199	  skill's SKILL.md, wraps it in an activation note — *"Treat its instructions as active
   200	  guidance for the duration of this session"* — and appends it to the agent's ephemeral
   201	  **system prompt** (server.py:3753–3766). This is not a model-chosen load; it's forced
   202	  guidance, in force from turn 1. **Proven live**: the model named the skill and quoted
   203	  its content with tools forbidden.
   204	- Granularity: **per process**, not per `session.create` call (the env is read at agent
   205	  build inside that process). There is no per-session skills param on `session.create`.
   206	  So role separation = one gateway child per role (or per run) with different env — which
   207	  is precisely how "the kanban flips a plain hermes chat into worker-mode": the kanban
   208	  worker is `hermes -p <profile> chat -q "…"` with role env vars, one process per run.
   209	- Because the skill rides the *ephemeral* prompt, it is **not persisted with the
   210	  session** — whoever resumes the session must supply the env again (proven: resume in a
   211	  skill-env process re-injected it). Our runtime must own "which role skill belongs to
   212	  this session" and set the env on every child it spawns for it.
   213	- Unknown skill name → structured `error` event at build ("agent init failed: Unknown
   214	  skill(s): …"), before any model call.
   215	- Relevant kanban history (docs/kanban-guide/03…/prompt-injection.md): the kanban
   216	  *abandoned* its force-loaded `kanban-worker` skill and folded the mandatory protocol
   217	  into an always-injected system-prompt block (`KANBAN_GUIDANCE`, gated on
   218	  `HERMES_KANBAN_TASK`), because "a rule the model can fail to load is not a rule."
   219	  The preload mechanism we're using here is the same *shape* as their fix — skill text
   220	  forced into the system prompt — so we get the unskippable property without patching
   221	  hermes-agent. Their per-task `--skills` flag (same underlying mechanism) remains
   222	  supported for opt-in extras.
   223	
   224	### Q3 — System prompt at kickoff: YES, but the same per-process/per-home granularity
   225	
   226	- `agent.system_prompt` in the active home's `config.yaml` is read at agent build and
   227	  passed (concatenated with any preloaded-skill text) as the agent's ephemeral system
   228	  prompt (server.py:3750–3766). Per-agent = per-`HERMES_HOME` (or per gateway process via
   229	  which home you point it at). No per-session RPC param exists.
   230	- Practical consequence: role framing belongs **in the role skill** (one lever, proven);
   231	  the config `system_prompt` is a home-wide baseline. Per-*ticket* dynamic context (ids,
   232	  paths) belongs in the next-step prompt text and/or per-run env — kanban does exactly
   233	  this (`HERMES_KANBAN_TASK` env + a minimal spawn prompt + tools that return context).
   234	- Also available: a `personality` overlay (`config.set` / `slash.exec /personality`)
   235	  mutates the live agent's ephemeral prompt but injects a visible pivot marker into
   236	  history — not suitable for role framing.
   237	
   238	### Q4 — Slash-command enumeration: YES
   239	
   240	- `commands.catalog` (live-tested) returns the full registry: 135 commands as
   241	  `[name, description]` pairs, grouped `categories`, a `canon` alias→canonical map,
   242	  `sub` (per-command subcommand lists), plus 62 skill-commands scanned from the skills
   243	  library, plus user `quick_commands`. Everything a UI needs to render a slash menu.
   244	- `command.resolve` canonicalizes a name; `slash.exec {session_id, command}` executes
   245	  (live-tested with `/status`; heavier commands run through a persistent per-session
   246	  `slash_worker.py` subprocess the gateway manages itself). `complete.slash` exists;
   247	  returned `items: []` for a valid prefix in my test — didn't chase it; the catalog is
   248	  the enumeration mechanism, completion is cosmetic.
   249	
   250	### Q5 — Run boundary & the approval cycle: YES — code observes start and end structurally
   251	
   252	- **Start**: `prompt.submit` returns `{"status": "streaming"}` immediately and
   253	  `message.start` is emitted. **End**: exactly one `message.complete` per run with
   254	  `status ∈ {complete, interrupted, error}` plus the full final text and usage. Observed
   255	  live for complete and interrupted; error path verified in source
   256	  (server.py:6770–6805) and via the `error` event for build failures. The doc's claim
   257	  holds: the transport gives structured start/finish/error.
   258	- **One in-flight run per session** enforced *within a gateway process* (`4009 session
   259	  busy` — proven). It does NOT span processes: two children could resume the same stored
   260	  key concurrently. The already-decided per-session serialized queue in our server is
   261	  therefore load-bearing correctness, not just ergonomics.
   262	- **Re-promptability**: after `message.complete` (including after interrupt), the same
   263	  session takes the next `prompt.submit` cleanly — proven. This is the entire mechanical
   264	  basis for "every run ends awaiting approval; approval later re-prompts": the approval
   265	  layer lives wholly in our code between two prompts; nothing approval-shaped ever
   266	  reaches the model. `session.steer` additionally injects mid-run text without breaking
   267	  the turn (source-verified).
   268	- **`approval.request`/`approval.respond`** exist but are a different layer: in-run
   269	  tool-permission gates (dangerous shell commands etc.), emitted only when Hermes
   270	  approvals are on. This machine runs `approvals.mode: off` (sessions report
   271	  `yolo: true`), so they never fire. Our planner gate does not need them; if we ever
   272	  want in-run tool gating for planner minds, it's a config switch in the planner home
   273	  plus handling that event.
   274	- Bonus boundary signal: with a child-per-run topology (below), the child's process exit
   275	  is a second, OS-level end-of-run signal — belt and braces for System B.
   276	
   277	---
   278	
   279	## 3 · Decisions (proposed)
   280	
   281	1. **Adopt Option 3 as specified — it is proven.** Stdio child on Hermes's own
   282	   interpreter; JSON-RPC; structured events; no venv coupling; resume across restarts
   283	   works. The in-process mount (old viewer: `from tui_gateway import server` inside its
   284	   own process) and the WS daemon are both unnecessary for v2; WS remains a pure
   285	   transport swap later (`tui_gateway/ws.py` drives the same `dispatch`).
   286	2. **Role skills at kickoff via `HERMES_TUI_SKILLS` on the child env — adopt.** It's the
   287	   same forced-system-prompt shape the kanban converged on, it's loud on failure, and
   288	   it's re-applied on every resume. Accept the consequence: role → process, so the
   289	   runtime spawns gateway children with role-specific env rather than multiplexing all
   290	   roles through one child.
   291	3. **Child-per-run topology (kanban-shaped), not one long-lived shared gateway.**
   292	   System B spawns a child for a step: spawn → ready (~0.1s) → `session.resume` (or
   293	   create) → `prompt.submit` → drain events → `message.complete` → close stdin → child
   294	   exits. Rationale: per-run env gives us per-ticket context injection (the kanban
   295	   pattern: `HERMES_KANBAN_TASK` et al.), role skills per run, no long-lived child to
   296	   supervise, process-exit as a backup end signal, and prompt-cache survives across
   297	   processes anyway (proven). ~1s overhead per step is noise against multi-minute steps;
   298	   chat gets the same path (a chat message is just a producer into the session's queue).
   299	   If chat latency ever matters, a kept-warm child per active session is a pure
   300	   optimization on the same primitive.
   301	4. **Role framing lives in the role skill; per-ticket specifics live in the next-step
   302	   prompt (+ per-run env).** Don't build a per-session system-prompt mechanism that
   303	   Hermes doesn't offer; don't use the personality overlay.
   304	5. **Approval machinery stays entirely in our code** — the gateway's approval events are
   305	   not our gate and stay off. The gate = System B observing `message.complete`, running
   306	   the proposal-present check, stamping status, and later re-prompting. Nothing new
   307	   needed from the gateway; this is exactly the decided design and the transport
   308	   supports it.
   309	6. **Dedicated `HERMES_HOME` for planner minds — recommended, needs owner sign-off.**
   310	   Proven that a separate home fully isolates sessions/config/skills (and that it needs
   311	   model config + credentials provisioned into it, or the build fails loudly). Keeps
   312	   planner sessions out of the owner's real session list, lets us pin
   313	   model/approvals/system_prompt/skills for planner agents without touching the personal
   314	   config, and gives our v2 role skills a home we own. Fallback: default home + v2 skills
   315	   installed under `~/.hermes/skills/` — works (probe 2 used the default home) but
   316	   mingles state.
   317	
   318	---
   319	
   320	## 4 · The plan — the agent-operation primitive
   321	
   322	One module in v2 (say `src/planner/minds/gateway.py` + `runner.py`), no Hermes imports:
   323	
   324	**a) Process layer (`GatewayChild`).** Spawn
   325	`<hermes>/venv/bin/python -m tui_gateway.entry` with pipes,
   326	`HERMES_PYTHON_SRC_ROOT=<hermes root>`, cwd = a per-run workspace dir, plus the env below.
   327	Reader thread turns stdout lines into frames (responses keyed by `id`; `method:"event"`
   328	frames onto a per-run event stream); stderr tailed into our run log. Wait for
   329	`gateway.ready` (timeout ~10s). Shutdown = close stdin, `wait()` with a short grace,
   330	then kill. Discovery of the hermes root/interpreter: config value with the
   331	`~/.hermes/hermes-agent/venv/bin/python` default, validated at boot by a cheap
   332	launch-and-ready smoke check.
   333	
   334	**b) Env assembly (the role parameterization).**
   335	- `HERMES_TUI_SKILLS=planner-worker | planner-rollover | planner-main` — the role skill
   336	  (v2-namespaced skills we author, installed in the active home's skills dir).
   337	- `HERMES_HOME=<planner home>` (per Decision 6).
   338	- Per-ticket context env for the CLI the skill tells the mind to use (e.g.
   339	  `PLANNER_TICKET_ID`, `PLANNER_API_URL`) — mirroring kanban's `HERMES_KANBAN_*` pins.
   340	- Optionally `HERMES_TUI_TOOLSETS=<subset>` to slim the toolset (env lever confirmed in
   341	  source, server.py:2042; not live-tested).
   342	
   343	**c) Run primitive (what System B calls).**
   344	`run_step(session_key | None, role, prompt_text, on_event) → RunResult`:
   345	1. spawn child with role env; await `gateway.ready`;
   346	2. `session.create` (first step — persist returned `stored_session_id` on the
   347	   ticket/global row) or `session.resume {session_id: stored_key}`;
   348	3. `prompt.submit`; stream events (deltas → optional live chat UI; `tool.*` → run log);
   349	4. on `message.complete`: capture `{text, status, usage}`; on `error` event or child
   350	   death: `RunResult(errored)`;
   351	5. close stdin; reap child.
   352	Status mapping is System B's, exactly as decided: launch → `agent_working`; complete →
   353	proposal-present check → `awaiting_approval` or `errored`; interrupted/error → `errored`
   354	(or per policy). Kickoff is just step 0 through the same call.
   355	
   356	**d) Serialization.** The per-session queue (already DECIDED) sits in front of
   357	`run_step`: one in-flight run per `session_key`, chat messages and next-step prompts are
   358	both producers. This is mandatory — the gateway's busy guard does not span our
   359	child-per-run processes.
   360	
   361	**e) Chat.** Per-ticket chat = enqueue the human's text as a `run_step` on the ticket's
   362	session. Global chat = a singleton session with `role=planner-main`. Same primitive,
   363	different session row. Streaming deltas can be relayed to the UI from `on_event`.
   364	
   365	**f) Slash menu.** On demand (or cached at boot): spawn/borrow a child,
   366	`commands.catalog`, cache `{pairs, categories, canon, sub}` for the UI. Invocation via
   367	`slash.exec {session_id, command}` on the session's child during a run window, through
   368	the same queue.
   369	
   370	**g) Interrupt/steer (operator controls).** While a run is live its child is known:
   371	`session.interrupt` (proven: yields `message.complete{status:interrupted}`, session
   372	stays usable) and `session.steer` map directly to stop/nudge buttons if wanted.
   373	
   374	**h) Hygiene + supervision.** Sessions never prompted leave no rows. Deleting a ticket
   375	can `session.delete` its stored key (must not be live). A boot-time smoke check
   376	(launch → ready → `commands.catalog` → exit) pins the private-surface risk: if a Hermes
   377	update moves anything we depend on, v2 says so at startup, loudly, instead of failing
   378	mid-run.
   379	
   380	**Follow-up tickets that fall out:** (1) `GatewayChild` + run primitive + queue with a
   381	fake-gateway test double (feed it canned frames) and one real-gateway smoke test;
   382	(2) the three role skills (worker first — port the intent from v1's productivity skills
   383	and the kanban worker-protocol lessons); (3) planner-home provisioning script (config +
   384	creds + skills sync) if Decision 6 is accepted; (4) System B wiring onto `RunResult`.
   385	
   386	---
   387	
   388	## 5 · Open sub-questions
   389	
   390	1. **Planner home provisioning (Decision 6).** What exactly must be copied/linked into a
   391	   dedicated `HERMES_HOME` for a working build (model config, `.env`/auth, skills)?
   392	   Settled by: a 30-minute provisioning probe — scaffold the home, copy the model block +
   393	   creds, prove one round trip. (Blocked only on the owner accepting Decision 6.)
   394	2. **Toolset slimming.** Does `HERMES_TUI_TOOLSETS` cleanly cut the 32k-token toolset
   395	   baseline, and what's the minimal set a worker needs (terminal for our CLI + file)?
   396	   Settled by: one live probe with the env set, checking `session.info.tools` + usage.
   397	3. **`message.complete` uniqueness under edge cases.** Auto-compression mid-run rotates
   398	   the session key (`_sync_session_key_after_compress`); goal-mode can chain turns. We
   399	   don't use goal mode, and resume follows the rotation chain (source-verified), but the
   400	   runner should treat "first `message.complete` after our submit" as the boundary and
   401	   re-read the stored key after each run. Settled by: a long-session integration test
   402	   once the runner exists.
   403	4. **`complete.slash` returned empty** for a valid prefix — harmless (catalog covers
   404	   enumeration), but worth one look if we want inline completion in chat.
   405	5. **Version pinning.** The gateway surface is an internal API (68 methods in the old
   406	   planner's notes, 85 now; core methods unchanged). The boot smoke check covers
   407	   detection; do we also want to pin the hermes-agent version v2 was validated against
   408	   and warn on drift? Settled by: owner preference.

