    25	| `message.start` is emitted with **no payload key** | `server.py:6512,6555,6592` (`_emit("message.start", sid)`) |
    26	| `message.delta` payload `{"text": str}` | `server.py:6718` |
    27	| Long handlers run on a 4-thread pool; `dispatch` returns `None` for them and the pool worker writes the response later → **responses can arrive out of order and interleave arbitrarily with events**; correlation MUST be by `id` | `server.py:170-188` (`_LONG_HANDLERS`, includes `session.resume`), `server.py:191-201` (pool), `server.py:914-950` (dispatch), `entry.py:337-342` |
    28	| Handler exception → `-32000 "handler error: ..."`; bad request → `-32600`/`-32602`; unknown method → `-32601` | `server.py:944`, `server.py:883-899`, `server.py:908-911` |
    29	| `session.create` result: `{"session_id": <8-hex live handle>, "stored_session_id": <durable YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS_xxxxxx>, "message_count", "messages", "info": {...}}`; slot limit → `4090` | `server.py:4303-4433` (sid `uuid4().hex[:8]` at 4305, result at 4413-4419, 4090 at 4355-4357) |
    30	| `session.create` writes **no DB row**; the row appears lazily on first prompt | `server.py:4393-4398` |
    31	| `session.resume {"session_id": <stored key>}` → `{"session_id": <NEW live handle>, "resumed": <durable key>, "message_count", "messages", ...}`; unknown key → `4007 "session not found"`; resume **follows the compression chain** so `resumed` may be a *newer* durable key than the one passed | `server.py:4555-4620` (4007 at 4599, chain at 4612-4619), `payload["resumed"]` at 4633/4725 |
    32	| `prompt.submit {"session_id": <live sid>, "text": ...}` → inline `{"status":"streaming"}`; busy → `4009 "session busy"`; the run itself happens on a daemon thread after the response | `server.py:6318-6384` (4009 at 6331-6332, result at 6384) |
    33	| Exactly one `message.complete` per run: payload `{"text": str, "usage": {...}, "status": "complete"|"interrupted"|"error"}` + optional `"reasoning"`/`"warning"`/`"rendered"` | `server.py:6770-6805` (status at 6771-6775, payload at 6795-6802, emit at 6805) |
    34	| Agent-init failure (bad `HERMES_TUI_SKILLS`, missing provider) arrives as an `error` **event** `{"message": "agent init failed: ..."}` — possibly before/without any prompt response | `server.py:1094`, `server.py:6365-6380`, spike §1 lines 110-113, 138-141 |
    35	| Other events in play: `session.info`, `thinking.delta`, `reasoning.available`, `tool.start`/`tool.complete`/`tool.generating`, `status.update` | `server.py:2826/2983/3030/3224/3048-3052/825-838` |
    36	| Stray library prints can't corrupt stdout: gateway redirects Python stdout to stderr; real stdout is JSON-only | `server.py:203-207` |
    37	| Child env: `HERMES_PYTHON_SRC_ROOT` (sys.path guard), `HERMES_TUI_SKILLS` (role skill(s), read at every agent build in that process), `HERMES_HOME` (planner home) | `entry.py:4-12`, spike §2 (Q2), spike §1 isolation probe |
    38	| Spawn: `<hermes_python> -m tui_gateway.entry`, PIPE stdin/stdout/stderr, text mode, line-buffered | spike §1 lines 24-29 |
    39	| Error codes in play: `-32700` parse, `-32600`/`-32601`/`-32602`, `-32000`, `4007` not found, `4009` busy, `4023` delete-while-active, `4090` slot limit | verified above + spike §2 Q1 |
    40	
    41	---
    42	
    43	## 1 · Design decisions (the load-bearing choices)
    44	
    45	**D1 — Injection seam = a `ChildProcess` Protocol + a `SpawnFn` spawn hook.**
    46	`gateway.py` defines a `ChildProcess` `typing.Protocol` (blocking line-oriented stdout/stderr
    47	reads, a stdin line write, close/kill/wait) and a `SpawnFn = Callable[[list[str], dict[str, str]],
    48	ChildProcess]`. `GatewayChild` takes `spawn: SpawnFn = spawn_popen`; `run_step` and
    49	`boot_smoke_check` thread the same parameter through. The fake (`fake.py`) implements
    50	`ChildProcess` and offers itself via `fake.spawn`. Consequence: the fake replaces ONLY the OS
    51	process; every frame it produces flows through GatewayChild's real reader thread, router,
    52	pending-request table, and event queue — the exact code path a real child exercises. Nothing in
    53	runner/queue knows whether the child is real.
    54	
    55	**D2 — One reader thread per child; responses by id-keyed pending table; events on a Queue.**
    56	A single daemon thread reads stdout lines and routes: response frames resolve entries in a
    57	lock-guarded `dict[int, _Pending]`; event frames go onto an unbounded `queue.Queue`;
    58	`gateway.ready` sets a ready gate instead of being enqueued (it is process-level, not
    59	session-level, and has no `session_id` — entry.py:316-322). EOF fails all pending waiters, wakes
    60	ready-waiters, and enqueues a `None` sentinel so an event drainer wakes. A second daemon thread
    61	tails stderr into a bounded deque.
    62	
    63	**D3 — Queue key is a caller-owned, required, non-empty string; `None` session_key never reaches
    64	the queue.** `MindQueue.submit(key: str, item)` — the key is the *mind's* stable identity (wave 3
    65	will pass e.g. `"ticket:17"` or `"global"`), not the Hermes session key. Justification: the
    66	serialization property must hold from step 0, when no durable session key exists yet — two queued
    67	step-0 producers for the same ticket MUST serialize (both would carry `session_key=None`, so
    68	keying on session_key cannot distinguish "same mind" from "different mind"). Treating each `None`
    69	as a unique bucket would permit exactly the concurrent-`session.create` race the queue exists to
    70	prevent (notes.md:99-106 — the gateway busy-guard is per-process only, our queue is the
    71	load-bearing correctness); lumping all `None`s into one bucket would falsely serialize unrelated
    72	minds. Only the caller knows mind identity, so the key is its input. `session_key: str | None`
    73	travels *inside* the queued item and is consumed by `run_step`.
    74	
    75	**D4 — Queue injects the run callable; the queue is pure serialization.**
    76	`MindQueue[T]` is generic over the item type and takes `run: Callable[[str, T], None]` at
    77	construction. Result delivery, status stamping, retries — all belong to the injected callable
    78	(wave 3's System B closure). Queue unit tests inject a controllable stub (Events + counters), so
    79	they never touch the fake gateway, let alone a real one.
    80	
    81	**D5 — No overall run wall-clock timeout.** The event drain in `run_step` blocks indefinitely by
    82	design (notes.md:184-185 REMOVE timeout-as-failure). Every terminal outcome has a structural wake:
    83	`message.complete`, an `error` event, or child EOF (the sentinel). The two *protocol handshake*
    84	phases keep injectable timeouts with sane defaults — `ready_timeout=10.0` (spike §4a) and
    85	`request_timeout=60.0` — because a gateway that never answers `gateway.ready` or an RPC is a
    86	transport failure, not a long-but-healthy run. Liveness-blind hangs are the PARKED reclaim
    87	question (notes.md:196-198), out of scope.
    88	
    89	**D6 — Smoke script lives at `src/planner/minds/smoke.py` with a `__main__` guard.**
    90	Rationale: `mypy` `files=["src"]` (pyproject.toml:42) type-checks it strictly alongside the
    91	package it exercises (scripts/ is outside mypy scope), `pytest` `testpaths=["tests"]`
    92	(pyproject.toml:47) never collects anything under src/ regardless of name, and the verify
    93	skip-scan only walks `tests/` (scripts/verify.py:119). Run as
    94	`.venv/bin/python -m planner.minds.smoke`. It is NOT imported by `__init__.py`.
    95	
    96	**D7 — `RunResult.session_key` always carries the freshest durable key the gateway reported.**
    97	Create path → `stored_session_id`; resume path → the response's `resumed` field (which may be a
    98	*newer* key than the one passed, because resume follows the compression-continuation chain,
    99	server.py:4612-4619 — spike sub-Q3). Failures before any key is learned return the input
   100	`session_key` unchanged (`None` on a failed create path). Notably: a `4009` on submit after a
   101	successful create still returns the new `stored_session_id`, so the caller can persist it.
   102	
   103	**D8 — `on_event` receives every event params-dict after ready, including terminal ones and
   104	unknown types.** One uniform rule: whatever the reader enqueued (the `params` dict:
   105	`{"type", "session_id"?, "payload"?}`) is forwarded before the runner acts on it. Consumers
   106	filter. Unknown event types are forwarded and otherwise ignored — never a crash. `on_event`
   107	exceptions are caught and logged; an observer must not kill a run.
   108	
   109	---
