I have enough to report. The protocol and runner mappings are clean; the queue contract is the part that needs correction before implementation, and the test plan currently follows the wrong abstraction.
codex
1. [severity: BLOCKER] D3 violates the ticket’s literal `session_key` invariant by keying serialization on a caller-owned mind id.
Evidence: `plan.md:63-73` says `MindQueue.submit(key)` uses `"ticket:17"`/`"global"`, “not the Hermes session key”; `plan.md:689-696` repeats that the key is not `session_key`.
Ticket requires “one in-flight run per `session_key`” in `queue.py` (`ticket.md:45-48`), and notes make this load-bearing because gateway `4009` is per-process only (`notes.md:99-106`).
The locking is race-free for the chosen key, but `plan.md:752-763` starts separate workers for different keys, so the same durable Hermes `session_key` submitted under two caller keys can overlap.

2. [severity: BLOCKER] The step-0 justification still permits two durable Hermes sessions for one mind.
Evidence: D3 says `session_key` travels inside the queued item (`plan.md:72-73`, `plan.md:694-696`).
`run_step` creates a new Hermes session whenever that captured value is `None` (`plan.md:599-607`).
So two step-0 items enqueued before the first result is persisted will serialize, but both can still call `session.create`; that contradicts “one durable Hermes session (`session_key`)” per mind (`notes.md:86-87`).

3. [severity: MAJOR] The test plan claims queue coverage but tests the wrong invariant.
Evidence: ticket requires “two producers on the SAME `session_key`” never overlap and different keys may overlap (`ticket.md:63-65`).
Planned tests use `"ticket:1"` / `"ticket:2"` queue keys (`plan.md:1141-1155`) and the coverage map calls that the session-key test (`plan.md:1199-1202`).
No planned test submits the same durable Hermes `session_key` under different caller keys, or two queued step-0 items that must converge to one persisted key.

Protocol-shape errors: clean.

Queue `_drain` / `submit` locking: clean for the chosen key; the violation is the chosen key’s contract.

`run_step` status mapping: clean.

Test hermeticity: clean.

mypy/ruff risks: clean.

Scope / owned files: clean.

Verdict: REPLAN.
